   236	
   237	## Independent-derivation check (Mathematica)
   238	
   239	The Mathematica and SymPy scripts share a near-identical pipeline:
   240	
   241	- Same `k = pi/(2 L)`.
   242	- Same `sigma_match` and `sigma_xi` definitions.
   243	- Same integration calls (`Integrate[sigmaMatch, {z, 0, lM}]` <-> `sp.integrate(sigma_match, (z, 0, L))`).
   244	- Same imported `g_-^F1` symbolic literal `(2*sqrt(4107 - 100*pi^2) - 37*sqrt(3))/(20*pi)`.
   245	- Same `Solve[gXi == gMinus, xi, Reals]` <-> `sp.solve(sp.Eq(g_xi, gminus), xi)[0]`.
   246	
   247	Variable-name divergences are cosmetic (`L` vs `lM`, `R` vs `rDisc`, `gminus` vs `gMinus`). The Mathematica script does add an explicit `If[!TrueQ[intervalCheck], fail[...]]` halt that SymPy omits (see F3).
   248	
   249	The underlying computation is just a definite integral and a `Solve`. The "independent derivation" bar is hard to clear when the claim is structurally that simple — both engines necessarily must compute the same integral. The mirroring is not gratuitous transliteration; both engines genuinely re-derive `g_match = pi/4` from the integral definition. I do not flag `mathematica_transliteration` here, but note that the .wl is structurally a tight mirror of the .py.
   250	
   251	## Engine cross-check
   252	
   253	Both engines produce (after normalization):
   254	
   255	- `sigma_match normalization = 1` (both)
   256	- `g_match = pi/4` (both)
   257	- `g_-^F1 = (-37*sqrt(3) + 2*sqrt(4107 - 100*pi^2))/(20*pi)` (both; same form modulo Mathematica's `Sqrt[]` vs SymPy's `sqrt`)
   258	- Numerical `g_match = 0.78539816339744830962` (SymPy) / `0.78539816339744830961566084581987572105` (Mathematica) — agree to all printed digits
   259	- Numerical `g_-^F1 = 0.75803507894466282692` (SymPy) / `0.75803507894466282691968089041411045778` (Mathematica) — agree
   260	- Numerical `xi_* = 0.18391840551153962831` (SymPy) / `0.18391840551153962830834428246014671147` (Mathematica) — agree
   261	- `g_xi(xi_*) - g_- = 0` (both `PASS`)
   262	- Interval check `True` (both)
   263	
